In C++ with multiple inheritance, declare virtual functions in base classes and override them in derived classes. For example:

class Base1 { public: virtual Base1* clone() const; };
class Base2 { public: virtual Base2* clone() const; };
class Derived : public Base1, public Base2 { public: virtual Derived* clone() const override; // Compiler may generate split functions or multiple entry points };

Implementation notes: The override in Derived must handle pointer adjustments. When returning from clone(), the compiler inserts thunks or adjusts the return value to point to the correct subobject, ensuring type safety and correct polymorphism.
